---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const eqCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · everything the business owns = assets', note: 'The resources a business controls: cash, inventory, machinery, vehicles, premises (syllabus 1.2). Every one of them had to be paid for somehow — and that "somehow" is the other side of the story.' },
    { math: '2 · each asset was funded two ways', note: 'Either outsiders supplied the funds — liabilities such as bank loans and trade payables — or the owner did. The owner\'s stake in the business is called capital, or owner\'s equity.' },
    { math: '3 · assets = liabilities + capital', note: 'The accounting equation. Not a formula to memorise but a fact to understand: a firm\'s resources must exactly equal the claims on those resources. It balances by construction — always.' },
    { math: '4 · owner invests $10,000 → both sides grow', note: 'Cash (an asset) rises by $10,000 and capital rises by $10,000. One transaction, two effects, equation still equal. This two-sided effect is what the double entry system (syllabus 2) records for every transaction.' },
    { math: '5 · van bought on credit → balance survives', note: 'A $5,000 van bought with a loan: assets +$5,000, liabilities +$5,000. This time the outsider funded the asset. Rearranged, the same equation tells you capital = assets − liabilities.' },
    { math: '6 · profit increases capital', note: 'When revenue exceeds expenses, the surplus stays in the business and the owner\'s stake grows; a loss shrinks it. That is why measuring profit or loss matters — it measures what trading added to (or took from) the owner (syllabus 1.1).' },
  ],
};

const acctLab = {
  params: [
    { name: 'assets', label: 'assets ($)', min: 0, max: 90000, step: 500, value: 80000, unit: '$' },
    { name: 'liabilities', label: 'liabilities ($)', min: 0, max: 90000, step: 500, value: 30000, unit: '$' },
    { name: 'revenue', label: 'revenue ($)', min: 0, max: 90000, step: 500, value: 50000, unit: '$' },
    { name: 'expenses', label: 'expenses ($)', min: 0, max: 90000, step: 500, value: 37000, unit: '$' },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'owner\'s equity = assets − liabilities', expr: 'assets - liabilities', unit: '$' },
    { label: 'profit for the year = revenue − expenses', expr: 'revenue - expenses', unit: '$' },
    { label: 'profit margin', expr: '(revenue - expenses)/revenue*100', unit: '%' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/accounting/foundational/the-fundamentals-of-accounting">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Accounting begins with two jobs that are easy to confuse. <strong>Book-keeping is the
    recording</strong>: capturing every transaction — each sale, purchase, receipt and payment —
    accurately and in order. <strong>Accounting is everything built on top of that
    record</strong>: checking it, summarising it into financial statements, and communicating
    the results to the people who use them. Cambridge IGCSE Accounting 0452 opens (section 1)
    with exactly this distinction, because the rest of the subject is meaningless without it:
    there is no accounting without book-keeping, but book-keeping without accounting is just a
    very tidy pile of evidence nobody has interpreted.
  </p>

  <h2>Why measure profit and loss at all</h2>
  <p>
    Syllabus section 1.1 asks you to explain the <em>purposes</em> of measuring business profit
    and loss. Three answers matter. First, <strong>to show whether trading is working</strong> —
    whether the activity of the business is adding to the owner's stake or quietly draining it.
    Second, <strong>to provide information for monitoring progress</strong>: this year's profit
    compared with last year's, with the plan, with a competitor's. Third, <strong>to support
    decision-making</strong>: whether to expand, borrow, change prices or close a product line
    are all judgements made on the numbers the accounting produces. Profit is not vanity — it is
    the scoreboard that lets a business be managed rather than merely run.
  </p>
  <p>
    The same section draws the book-keeping/accounting line, and the examiners like it drawn
    precisely. <strong>Book-keeping</strong> is the detailed recording of financial transactions
    — writing up the ledger accounts from invoices, receipts and till rolls. It is clerical,
    rule-following, and foundational. <strong>Accounting</strong> uses those recorded results to
    check and verify them (the trial balance, section 3), to summarise them into financial
    statements (section 5), and to interpret them for the interested parties who make decisions
    on them (section 6). A book-keeper records that $500 left the till; an accountant can tell
    you whether that $500 was an expense, an asset, or a sign of theft.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the accounting equation, built up step by step from what a business owns and who funded it" widget="equationStep" config={eqCfg} />

  <h2>The equation that never breaks</h2>
  <p>
    Syllabus section 1.2 introduces the three master terms. <strong>Assets</strong> are
    resources of value owned or controlled by the business. <strong>Liabilities</strong> are the
    amounts the business owes to outsiders. <strong>Owner's equity (capital)</strong> is the
    owner's stake — what the business would owe back to the owner if everything were sold and
    every debt paid. Because every asset must have been funded by one of the other two, the
    three terms lock into the accounting equation, and <em>every single transaction</em> — all
    of them, forever — keeps it balanced. Watch the steps above: the owner invests cash and both
    sides grow; a van is bought on credit and both sides grow again; rearranged, the equation
    hands you capital = assets − liabilities.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="drive the equation yourself — assets and liabilities set equity; revenue and expenses set profit" widget="sliderLab" config={acctLab} />

  <p>
    In the lab, the first readout is the equation rearranged: whatever you do to the sliders,
    equity is pinned at assets minus liabilities — the equation is a constraint, not a
    coincidence. The second and third readouts do the same for the income side: profit is
    revenue minus expenses, and the profit margin expresses that profit as a percentage of
    sales. Notice the two sliders are independent: a business can be profitable yet short of
    cash, and flush with cash yet loss-making — the difference between profit and liquidity is
    one of the first lessons accountants learn the hard way.
  </p>

  <h2>Where this leads</h2>
  <p>
    Everything else in the 0452 syllabus is scaffolding around this foundation. The double entry
    system (section 2) is the machine that records each transaction's two effects so the
    equation always holds. Verification (section 3) checks the records can be trusted. The
    financial statements (section 5) — the statement of profit or loss and the statement of
    financial position — are simply the equation and the profit calculation, laid out in the
    form users need. Master the fundamentals here and every later topic is an extension of the
    same two ideas: records must be complete and accurate, and the resources must always equal
    the claims.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Cambridge IGCSE Accounting 0452 syllabus for 2027–2029, section 1 "The fundamentals of accounting" (1.1 The purpose of accounting; 1.2 The accounting equation); Wood &amp; Sangster, <em>Business Accounting 1</em>; IASB, <em>Conceptual Framework for Financial Reporting</em> (2018).</p>
</TopicPage>
